  "clues": {
    "kinetics": "Food raises exposure about 3.4-fold with a low-fat meal and 5.1- to 5.3-fold with a high-fat one. It is highly protein bound and predominantly metabolized by CYP3A.",
    "substance": "A molecular weight of 868.44, with very low aqueous solubility.",
    "product": "Approved under an NDA as 10, 50 and 100 mg tablets, dosed once daily.",
    "safety": "Contraindicated with strong CYP3A inhibitors at initiation and during ramp-up. Neutropenia, headache and diarrhea are the most common reactions with acalabrutinib.",
    "indication": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia or small lymphocytic lymphoma, and newly diagnosed AML in adults 75 or older, with azacitidine, decitabine or low-dose cytarabine.",
    "mechanism": "Selectively inhibits BCL-2, displacing pro-apoptotic proteins such as BIM and restoring apoptosis."
  },
  "tell": "A 26-hour terminal half-life, and a food effect of more than fivefold, set this BCL-2 inhibitor apart.",
